{
  "id": "11416_yokan",
  "headword": "{予感|よかん}",
  "reading": "よかん",
  "part_of_speech": "noun / suru verb",
  "gloss": "premonition, hunch, presentiment",
  "schema_version": "2.0",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "premonition, hunch, presentiment",
      "explanation": "A feeling that something is about to happen, without having concrete evidence. Can be about both good and bad events."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "11416_yokan_ex1",
      "japanese": "{嫌|いや}な{予感|よかん}がする。",
      "english": "I have a bad feeling about this.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "11416_yokan_ex2",
      "japanese": "{何|なに}かいいことが{起|お}きる{予感|よかん}がした。",
      "english": "I had a feeling something good was going to happen.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "11416_yokan_ex3",
      "japanese": "{朝|あさ}から{嫌|いや}な{予感|よかん}がしていたが、やはり{試験|しけん}の{結果|けっか}は{悪|わる}かった。",
      "english": "I had a bad feeling since morning, and sure enough, the exam results were poor.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "COMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {予感|よかん}がする (to have a premonition)\n- {嫌|いや}な{予感|よかん} (a bad feeling)\n- {予感|よかん}が{的中|てきちゅう}する (a premonition comes true)\n- {予感|よかん}が{当|あ}たる (a hunch proves right)\n\nThe phrase {嫌|いや}な{予感|よかん}がする is extremely common and is often the first thing that comes to mind when Japanese speakers sense something will go wrong.",
